\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

% TODO: Experimental setup (datasets, judges, style axes, rewriting pipeline)

\subsection{Statistical Framework}
\label{sec:stat_framework}

We organize our statistical tests into two tiers.
The \emph{primary family} consists of the 9-cell grid in Table~\ref{tab:main_results} (3 judges $\times$ 3 style axes), corrected with Holm-Bonferroni to control family-wise error rate.
All other analyses---the NLI threshold sensitivity sweep (\S\ref{sec:nli_sensitivity}), consistency filter validation (\S\ref{sec:consistency}), and binarized Likert diagnostic---are classified as either \emph{exploratory} or \emph{confirmatory} and reported with uncorrected $p$-values, clearly labeled as such.

% TODO: Full experimental results (Table 2, main findings across judges and axes)

\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

\subsection{NLI Gate Validation}
\label{sec:nli_validation}

\paragraph{False negative rate.}
A reviewer raised concern about an apparent 67\% false negative rate (FNR) of the NLI gate.
This figure reflects the \emph{within-rejected} error rate: of 12 pairs that the NLI gate rejected (score $< 0.90$), 8 were judged content-preserving by a Qwen3-32B annotator.
The overall FNR is $8/95 = 8.4\%$, where 95 is the total number of annotator-positive pairs (87 NLI-pass + 8 NLI-fail).
The gate achieves precision of 98.9\% (only 1 of 88 NLI-pass pairs was judged content-altered) and recall of 91.6\%.
Three lines of evidence support interpreting the gate as a precision-oriented conservative filter rather than an unreliable classifier.

First, downstream results are robust to threshold relaxation.
Across NLI thresholds from 0.70 to 0.95, the formality win rate remains stable at 72.3--73.9\%, with all sign tests yielding $p < 0.005$ (\S\ref{sec:nli_sensitivity}).
Second, the consistency filter introduces no systematic selection bias: consistent ($n = 55$) and inconsistent ($n = 43$) pairs do not differ in NLI score (Mann-Whitney $p = 0.824$), BERTScore ($p = 0.12$), original text length ($p = 0.137$), or length difference ($p = 0.715$).
Third, among the 8 false negatives, the direction split is 6 formal-to-casual vs.\ 2 casual-to-formal (Fisher exact $p = 0.35$), ruling out directional bias in the gate's errors.
Qualitative inspection of the 8 FN cases (Appendix~\ref{app:fn_analysis}) reveals that 3--4 involve rewrites where the NLI gate correctly detected subtle content additions or prompt leakage that the LLM annotator overlooked, suggesting the gate may be \emph{more} conservative than the annotator rather than less reliable.

The NLI gate operates as a precision-oriented conservative filter: it sacrifices a bounded amount of recall (8.4\% false negative rate) to ensure near-zero contamination of the downstream analysis (precision = 98.9\%).

\paragraph{Threshold sensitivity.}
\label{sec:nli_sensitivity}
The NLI score distribution is bimodal, with the majority of pairs scoring either above 0.95 or below 0.30.
The 0.90 threshold lies on a plateau of this distribution, not a cliff edge: changing the threshold from 0.70 to 0.95 shifts the pass rate only from 89\% to 85\%, and the resulting formality win rates (72.3\% at 0.70, 73.9\% at 0.90) and significance levels (all $p < 0.005$) are stable.
The 0.90 threshold is consistent with standard DeBERTa-v3-large NLI calibration and was not optimized on downstream results.

\subsection{Measurement Sensitivity: Likert vs.\ Pairwise}
\label{sec:measurement}

\paragraph{Head-to-head comparison with RATE.}
Applying RATE's Likert ATE methodology \citep{reber2025rate} to our formality style-transformed pairs yields Cohen's $d = 0.10$, $p = 0.33$ (non-significant, $n = 88$); pairwise comparison on identical data reveals a 69.1\% formal preference with $p = 0.006$ ($n = 55$ position-consistent pairs)---directly demonstrating the Likert Blindspot.
The two approaches are complementary rather than contradictory: RATE measures aggregate attribute effects on reward model scores via Likert ATE, while CSD targets isolated per-axis style preference via pairwise comparison.
The Likert Blindspot implies that RATE's style-related findings may underestimate the true magnitude of attribute effects when integer scoring masks the underlying preference signal.

\paragraph{Precision of estimates.}
Following \citet{hoenig2001abuse}, we do not report post-hoc power calculations based on observed effect sizes, as such analyses are circular: observed power is a monotone function of the $p$-value and therefore adds no information beyond what the test already provides.
Instead, we report confidence interval width as a precision metric.
The pairwise formal win rate of 69.1\% has a Wilson 95\% CI of $[55.97\%, 79.72\%]$ (width = 23.75 percentage points), which excludes chance (50\%) and provides a practically informative bound on effect magnitude.

\subsection{Consistency Filter Robustness}
\label{sec:consistency}

The consistency filter retains 55 of 98 valid pairs (56.1\%) by discarding pairs where the judge's preference reverses across the two position-swapped trials.
To address the concern that discarding 43.9\% of pairs introduces selection bias, we compare consistent and inconsistent groups across four covariates and find no significant differences: NLI score (Mann-Whitney $p = 0.824$), BERTScore ($p = 0.12$), original text length ($p = 0.137$), and length difference ($p = 0.715$).
A Bradley-Terry analysis \citep{bradley1952rank} using all pairwise data---including position-inconsistent pairs---is planned as a robustness check that eliminates the need for consistency filtering entirely.

